module ar-circuit-court-civil.civil

start commenced

state commenced: "Action commenced" by applicant cite "Ark. R. Civ. P. 3(a)"
{
  note "A civil action is commenced by filing a complaint with the clerk of the court, who notes on it the date and precise time of filing (Ark. R. Civ. P. 3(a)). If service is not made on a defendant within 120 days after the filing of the complaint, or within an extension granted on written motion and good cause made within the 120 days or a previous extension, the action is dismissed as to that defendant without prejudice on motion or on the court's initiative; an order of extension must be entered within 30 days after the motion is filed, by the end of the 120-day period or by the end of the previous extension, whichever is later (Ark. R. Civ. P. 4(i)). The court may not extend the Rule 4(i) time except as that rule states (Ark. R. Civ. P. 6(b)). Periods in this package are stated, not counted: computation is Ark. R. Civ. P. 6(a). The filing fee is not stated in the vendored rules; its waiver is Ark. R. Civ. P. 72."
  edge-commenced-served "Defendant served" -> answer-due { authority cite "Ark. R. Civ. P. 4" }
  edge-commenced-unserved "Service not made within 120 days or an extension" -> dismissed-unserved {
    authority cite "Ark. R. Civ. P. 4(i)"
  }
}

terminal dismissed-unserved: "Dismissed without prejudice for untimely service" outcome [loss, dismissal] cite "Ark. R. Civ. P. 4(i)"
{
  note "Dismissal under Rule 4(i)(1) is without prejudice and as to the unserved defendant only. The subdivision does not apply to service in a foreign country under Rule 4(h) or to complaints against unknown tortfeasors (Ark. R. Civ. P. 4(i)(3))."
}

decision answer-due: "Answer, motion or default" by office cite "Ark. R. Civ. P. 12(a)"
{
  note "A defendant files an answer within 30 days after service of the summons and complaint; a defendant served by warning order under Rule 4(g)(3) or (4), within 30 days from the date of first publication or posting; a defendant incarcerated in a jail, penitentiary or other correctional facility in Arkansas, within 60 days after service. An answer or reply to a cross-claim or counterclaim is filed within 30 days after service. The court may on motion extend the time for any responsive pleading (Ark. R. Civ. P. 12(a)(1)). After remand from federal court, an adverse party has 30 days from receipt of notice of the filing of the remand order to answer or move (Ark. R. Civ. P. 12(a)(3)). The 3 business days Rule 6(d) adds after service by mail do not extend the time to answer or move when the summons and complaint are served by mail or commercial delivery company under Rule 4 (Ark. R. Civ. P. 6(d)). No leading decision is named."
  edge-answer-due-default "No response" -> default-entered {
    authority cite "Ark. R. Civ. P. 55(a)"
  }
  edge-answer-due-motion "Rule 12(b) motion filed" -> pleading-motion {
    authority cite "Ark. R. Civ. P. 12(b)"
  }
  edge-answer-due-answer "Answer filed" -> discovery { authority cite "Ark. R. Civ. P. 26" }
}

decision pleading-motion: "Motion to dismiss decided" by court cite "Ark. R. Civ. P. 12(b)"
{
  note "The defenses that may be made by motion include failure to state facts upon which relief can be granted and the pendency of another action between the same parties arising out of the same transaction or occurrence; a motion under (b)(6) presenting matters outside the pleading that the court does not exclude is treated as one for summary judgment (Ark. R. Civ. P. 12(b)). A response in opposition and a reply are made as Rules 6(c) and 7(b) provide (Ark. R. Civ. P. 12(i)): a written motion and notice of its hearing are served not later than 20 days before the hearing, a response within 10 days after service of the motion, and a reply within 5 days after service of the response, unless the court orders otherwise or another rule fixes a different period (Ark. R. Civ. P. 6(c)). If the court denies the motion or postpones it to trial, the responsive pleading is filed within 10 days after notice of the court's action (Ark. R. Civ. P. 12(a)(2)(A)). No leading decision is named."
  edge-pleading-motion-dismissed "Granted" -> dismissed-pleading {
    authority cite "Ark. R. Civ. P. 12(b)"
  }
  edge-pleading-motion-denied "Denied: responsive pleading within 10 days after notice" -> discovery {
    authority cite "Ark. R. Civ. P. 12(a)"
  }
}

terminal default-entered: "Default judgment" outcome [loss] cite "Ark. R. Civ. P. 55(a)"
{
  note "When a party against whom affirmative relief is sought has failed to plead or otherwise defend, judgment by default may be entered by the court (Ark. R. Civ. P. 55(a)). A party who has appeared is served with written notice of the application at least 3 days before the hearing on it (Ark. R. Civ. P. 55(b)). The court may on motion set aside a default judgment for mistake, inadvertence, surprise or excusable neglect, voidness, fraud or misconduct of an adverse party, or any other reason justifying relief; the movant must show a meritorious defense unless the judgment is void (Ark. R. Civ. P. 55(c)). Outcome from the defending side."
}

terminal dismissed-pleading: "Dismissed on the motion" outcome [win, dismissal] cite "Ark. R. Civ. P. 12(b)"

state discovery: "Discovery" by applicant cite "Ark. R. Civ. P. 26"
{
  note "Discovery is governed by Ark. R. Civ. P. 26 through 37. The court may direct a pretrial conference (Ark. R. Civ. P. 16). A party demands a jury in writing at any time after commencement and not later than 20 days before the trial date; when a demand covers only some issues, any other party may demand a jury on the others within 10 days after service of the demand; failure to file the demand waives a jury (Ark. R. Civ. P. 38(a)-(c)). Where there has been no action shown on the record for the past 12 months, or a failure to comply with the rules or an order, the court sends notice that the case will be dismissed for want of prosecution unless application is made on a stated day, upon good cause, to continue it (Ark. R. Civ. P. 41(b))."
  edge-discovery-sj "Summary judgment motion filed" -> sj-motion {
    authority cite "Ark. R. Civ. P. 56(c)"
  }
  edge-discovery-trial "No dispositive motion" -> trial { authority cite "Ark. R. Civ. P. 16" }
  edge-discovery-settle "Settled or voluntarily nonsuited" -> settled {
    authority cite "Ark. R. Civ. P. 41(a)"
  }
}

decision sj-motion: "Summary judgment decided" by court cite "Ark. R. Civ. P. 56(c)"
{
  note "A claimant may move after 20 days from the commencement of the action or after service of the adverse party's motion; absent leave of court for good cause, any party files a summary judgment motion no later than 45 days before any scheduled trial date (Ark. R. Civ. P. 56(a), (b)). The adverse party serves a response within 21 days after the motion is served, and the movant may reply within 14 days after the response is served; the court may hold a hearing not less than 7 days after the time for a reply (Ark. R. Civ. P. 56(c)(1)). Judgment is rendered if there is no genuine issue as to any material fact and the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law (Ark. R. Civ. P. 56(c)(2)). No leading decision is named."
  edge-sj-motion-granted "Granted" -> judgment-for-movant { authority cite "Ark. R. Civ. P. 56(c)" }
  edge-sj-motion-denied "Denied or partly denied" -> trial {
    authority cite "Ark. R. Civ. P. 56(d)"
  }
}

terminal judgment-for-movant: "Judgment for the movant" outcome [win, judgment] cite "Ark. R. Civ. P. 56(c)"
{
  note "Partial summary judgment and an order specifying facts without substantial controversy (Ark. R. Civ. P. 56(d)) are not modeled."
}

state trial: "Trial" by court cite "Ark. R. Civ. P. 39"
{
  note "A party may move for a directed verdict at the close of the opponent's evidence or of all the evidence, stating its specific grounds; in a jury trial, a party without the burden of proof must so move at the close of all the evidence to preserve a sufficiency challenge for appeal (Ark. R. Civ. P. 50(a), (e)). In a contested nonjury action the court finds the facts specially and states its conclusions of law if a party requests it before entry of judgment (Ark. R. Civ. P. 52(a))."
  edge-trial-post "Verdict or decision; judgment entered" -> post-trial {
    authority cite "Ark. R. Civ. P. 58"
  }
}

decision post-trial: "Post-trial motions decided" by court cite "Ark. R. Civ. P. 59(b)"
{
  note "A motion for judgment notwithstanding the verdict is filed not later than 10 days after entry of judgment (Ark. R. Civ. P. 50(b)(2)); a motion to amend findings, not later than 10 days after entry (Ark. R. Civ. P. 52(b)(1)); a motion for a new trial, not later than 10 days after entry (Ark. R. Civ. P. 59(b)). A motion made before entry is treated as filed on the day after entry, and one the court neither grants nor denies within 30 days is deemed denied as of the 30th day (Ark. R. Civ. P. 50(b)(2), 52(b)(1), 59(b)). Opposing affidavits on a new trial motion are filed within 10 days after service, extendable by not more than 20 days (Ark. R. Civ. P. 59(d)). The court may not extend these times except as the rules state (Ark. R. Civ. P. 6(b)). The court may modify or vacate a judgment within ninety days of its filing with the clerk (Ark. R. Civ. P. 60(a)). A motion for attorney's fees is filed no later than 14 days after entry of judgment unless a statute or order provides otherwise (Ark. R. Civ. P. 54(e)(2))."
  edge-post-trial-judgment "Motions resolved or none filed" -> judgment-entered {
    authority cite "Ark. R. Civ. P. 58"
  }
}

state judgment-entered: "Final judgment entered" by office cite "Ark. R. Civ. P. 58"
{
  note "Every judgment or decree is set forth on a separate document and is effective only when so set forth and entered as provided in Administrative Order No. 2; entry is not delayed for the taxing of costs (Ark. R. Civ. P. 58). Administrative Order No. 2 is not vendored. A judgment or order is entered for appeal purposes when filed under Administrative Order No. 2(b) (Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 4(d))."
  edge-judgment-entered-appeal "Next step" -> appeal-decision {
    authority cite "Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 4(a)"
  }
}

decision appeal-decision: "Notice of appeal within 30 days of entry?" by applicant cite "Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 4(a)"
{
  note "A notice of appeal is filed within 30 days from the entry of the judgment, decree or order appealed from, or of the Rule 54(b) certificate; a notice of cross-appeal within the later of 10 days after the notice of appeal is filed or 30 days from entry; a notice filed after the decision is announced but before entry is treated as filed on the day after entry (Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 4(a)). A timely motion under Ark. R. Civ. P. 50(b), 52(b) or 59(a), or another motion to vacate, alter or amend the judgment made no later than 10 days after entry, extends the time for all parties to 30 days from entry of the order disposing of the last outstanding motion or from the day it is deemed denied, the 30th day after its filing (Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 4(b)(1)). On a showing of failure to receive notice of the judgment, the circuit court, on motion filed within 180 days of entry, extends the time by 14 days from entry of the extension order (Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 4(b)(3))."
  edge-appeal-decision-taken "Notice filed" -> appeal-taken {
    authority cite "Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 3"
  }
  edge-appeal-decision-none "No notice filed" -> final-no-appeal {
    authority cite "Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 4(a)"
  }
}

terminal appeal-taken: "Appeal taken: continue in the Arkansas appellate package" outcome [appeal] cite "Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 3"
{
  note "Hand-off to lit/arkctapp (ar-appellate-civil)."
}

terminal final-no-appeal: "Final: no appeal" outcome [final] cite "Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 4(a)"

terminal settled: "Settled or voluntarily nonsuited" outcome [settlement] cite "Ark. R. Civ. P. 41(a)"
{
  note "A plaintiff may dismiss without prejudice before final submission of the case to the jury, or to the court in a bench trial; the dismissal is a matter of right but is effective only upon entry of a court order. A second voluntary dismissal of an action based on or including the same claim operates as an adjudication on the merits unless all parties stipulate in writing otherwise (Ark. R. Civ. P. 41(a)(1), (2))."
}
